      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Extrusive landforms are formed on the Earth's surface and include volcanic cones, lava plateaus, and calderas.",
        "Intrusive landforms are formed when magma cools and solidifies beneath the Earth's crust.",
        "Batholiths are massive plutonic intrusive bodies.",
        "Laccoliths are dome-shaped intrusions with a pipe-like conduit below.",
        "Sills represent horizontal sheets of cooled magma, while dikes are near-vertical intrusions."
